n this and you know if you or  anyone you know is interested in  interesting data rolls in here or in  Seattle or for remotes  using data to build ml products and  track and move money I'd be happy to  talk to you about that as well how do  you deal with the features which are  used in all these pipelines like they  would be like tons of features how do  users get those features yeah so that's  that's an excellent question which is  the talk into itself one of the things  we spent a lot of time working on the  past year or two is having like sort of  the  right abstraction for people to define  features where they only have to write  code once and it can be available for  training for batch jobs and for online  jobs and so that you can potentially if  it makes sense like share features  between applications and so we've done a  lot of work to kind of build a DSL for  that that has different compilers or  backends that can run like on spark for  maybe your training or on kind of like a  Kafka consumer set up for real time but  where the future code is the same and  that does a lot allow a lot of what you  said where you can almost like sort of  browse the features and be able to use  the same features in different models  which has been really powerful for us  especially since we often have like even  in the case of risk management there's a  lot of sort of related applications  where you might want similar features  and inputs um a few slides back you kind  of had your dashboard where you showed  failed shipped etc and you mentioned you  know the iterative process where you're  kind of comparing results of models and  then if it was not if it was better than  the other ones and it would ship so I'm  wondering maybe you don't always want if  it's something's better than the  previous model you might not always want  to ship it to production because it's  still something you're kind of  evaluating so how do you hold that  that's a really good question  and it's also something that's like a  little bit different for each  application and we're trying now to kind  of capture that more flexibly and sort  of like an evaluation API where you tell  us like what are the metrics you care  about and how they connect so in the  case of some of our applications people  do things like they want to say  something like maximize the recall at a  fixed false discovery or false positive  rate and if you meet that for any given  model which it might be some kind of  global model it might be segmented for a  user or particular users then that's  like ship it right and then the second  part of what you said maybe there's a  little bit of definition around like  what is ship it mean like are you going  straight out to production or how do you  manage that roll out so that's something  right now that mostly applications  manages themselves we're like usually  they'll kind of push the updated model  to almost like a candidate tag  and then do some kind of like feature  flag or a be testing or you know maybe  in the future and for some application  some kind of bandit but that's something  that eventually we kind of like hope to  offer a little bit more standardized  functionality as well um how do you  solve the problem of one job and  cannibalizing all the resource cpu ram  causing especially if it's over a  weekend it's causing deletes for other  jobs yeah so that's a lot better with  communities because you can do things  like set the parameters they give you  for this or things like this toleration  affinity of sort of like I want this job  to run on these nodes and don't run  other jobs there so it's not exactly one  to one but it gives you like a lot more  control and isolation for your  particular job like I mentioned we do  also have you know we set hard and soft  limits and sometimes people go over  those because we let them do like  basically arbitrary things so there's a  little bit of tuning around that that  overall I think the rate of like  something being killed because it's like  using too much memory is like pretty low  at this point  and so it's something that we're able to  work with more ease a lot more easily  than previously  how do you handle dependencies let's say  you have one of these embedding models  that somebody we trained and yeah  hundreds of other models depend on it  how does your API handle that yeah so  that's an excellent question and I guess  that that's mostly outside the API but  it's something that we deal a lot with  and so one thing that's maybe  interesting in this slide this diorama  ID that's a sha-256  so it's it's like sort of a hash of the  bytes and so we we have kind of like  this notion of immutable models so  anytime something is different you're  gonna have to update that sha and that's  one thing that keeps you from like  stomping over something else we also  have kind of what we call like a concept  of meta models where you can sort of  like compose different models so you  could have like model a either it  depends on model B or maybe it sells out  so maybe you have a case where it's like  you know use this model if there's not a  specific model for the user if there is  then shell out to that  all of that will be specified kind of  like using these immutable Shaw's so  basically that kind of prevents us from  getting in a situation where this thing  changed underneath you and you didn't  realize it and then the thing that's  mutable is just that AG - Shaw mapping  where the tag is your application so in  the end you say like you know radar  currently points to this Shaw and then  that Shaw may actually like shell out to  some number of other Shaw's as well  [Music]


